comments[5]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-13T22:00:29.132Z",codex-root,"Duplicate check 2026-07-13: pm-e9zh owns terminal resolution evidence and completed audits inspected history,but no open task owns typed terminal relationship reconstruction in changelog-safe epoch batches; this task is downstream and coordinated,not duplicate."
  "2026-07-26T17:22:12.268Z","harness:claude-code","Wave 2 is executing. This comment records the method and the boundary, so the remaining scope on this item stays clear.\n\nThe ordering constraint this item was waiting on is gone. Release attribution is now a stamped field on 1,782 of 1,795 terminal items rather than a function of updated_at, so a metadata write on a closed item is changelog-neutral. That was the entire reason terminal enrichment was deferred, and it was verified rather than assumed — see pm-3j6it6.\n\nThe tranche executing under pm-qudvto applies four evidence classes in strength order and nothing else: typed hierarchy mirror (implements to an existing parent), explicit pm identifiers recorded in the item's own durable text, shared linked file or doc, shared GitHub issue or pull request reference. Yields were 1,607 / 2,244 / 45 / 2 edges across 1,713 items. Every write carries its evidence class in the history message, so the enrichment is auditable and reversible per item.\n\nWhat this tranche deliberately does not do, and what therefore remains on this item:\n\n- Epoch partitioning. The pass ran as one bounded sweep over the whole terminal set rather than newest-to-oldest by historical epoch. That was safe here because no edge depends on ordering, but the epoch-ordered discipline this item specifies is untested and is the harder requirement at million-item scale.\n- Bounded resumability. The pass was restartable only because it is idempotent, not because it checkpoints. The demonstration of bounded resumability with non-overlapping claims is not done.\n- Ambiguous retyping and high-impact hubs. Nothing was retyped and no hub was restructured, so the human-review requirement was avoided rather than satisfied.\n- The residual. 38 terminal items have no evidence in any of the four classes. They are not fabricated links and not silently dropped; the programme-root subset of them is now owned by pm-1w6scp, which handles the thirteen duplicated v1 milestone epics and the parentless roots as a structural problem rather than an edge-inference one.\n"
  "2026-07-27T04:19:40.123Z","harness:claude-code","Evidence 2026-07-27: bounded lineage tranche executed under this item's evidence rules, with the method recorded so it is repeatable and auditable.\n\nScope. Two edge classes were recovered from durable text only, no inference, no heuristic similarity, no judgement per item. Both are explicit-identifier evidence, which this item's acceptance criteria name as an admissible class.\n\nClass one, supersedes, 39 edges. Extraction required a directional phrase immediately followed by an item identifier, in one of five forms: this item is superseded by X, folded into X, duplicate_of X, and the two forward spellings X supersedes Y and X replaces Y. The overwhelming majority, 32 of 39, come from a single machine-written pattern left by the 2026-04-04 dedupe pass, whose comments read verbatim that this item is superseded by pm-phpq for cluster release-loop::2026-03-07 and equivalents. Every referenced identifier was checked to resolve to an existing item file before any write; zero unresolved references. Corpus effect: supersedes 1 to 40.\n\nClass two, discovered_from, 28 edges. Extraction required one of sixteen derivation phrases within sixty characters of an identifier, the phrase set being discovered while, discovered during, discovered from, found while, surfaced while, filed while, filed from, filed during, split out of, split from, extracted from, carved out of, spun out of, follow-up to, follow-on to and uncovered while. Thirty-one candidates were produced; three were dropped by review because the sentence names the derived item rather than the source, which reverses the edge direction, and fabricating a reversed edge is precisely what this item's criteria forbid. Corpus effect: discovered_from 161 to 187.\n\nTotals. Edges 9262 to 9356 over 2121 nodes. No existing edge was removed or retyped in place; every write is additive and appears in each item's history with a message naming its evidence class, so each is individually reversible and explainable.\n\nWhat this deliberately did not do. No ordering edges were manufactured. The temptation is real, because the corpus has release cohorts and parent lineages from which a plausible sequence could be derived for hundreds of terminal items, and the resulting graph would look far deeper. That is fabrication under this item's rules and it was not done. The honest state is recorded instead as pm-gxs55a: 88.5 percent of sequenced nodes have no predecessor and the longest path is six hops, which on inspection turn out to be one item and five duplicates of it.\n\nResidual for this item. The extraction rules above are narrow by design and their yield is correspondingly small against 1799 closed items. Widening them is the work, and it needs the human review this item requires, because the next tier of evidence is prose that states a relationship without stating a direction.\n"
  "2026-07-27T13:01:10.030Z","harness:claude-code","Method result 2026-07-27, recorded here so no later batch repeats it: text-based ordering extraction over this corpus is exhausted, and the number is small enough to change the strategy.\n\nA full scan of all 2,131 item documents for explicit precedence statements, using 21 phrase forms in a single-endpoint pass and 9 in a two-endpoint pass with continuation handling, produced 44 distinct candidate pairs. Eleven were already edged. Ten survived review and were applied as blocked_by under pm-ye46ns. Twenty were dropped, a 67 percent refusal rate, in three classes: hub-note misattribution where a roadmap or pass-summary item describes edges between other items and the extractor attributes them to the item it is reading (14), continuation over-reach where a comma begins a new clause rather than extending a prerequisite list (2), and over-broad scope where a sentence gating part of an item is read as gating the item (4).\n\nThe same scan re-checked the two lineage kinds this backfill already harvested and confirms both are saturated. Thirty-five \"superseded by\" assertions exist and 51 of the 54 supersedes edges are applied; the three apparent gaps are hub-note misattribution. Thirteen candidate discovered_from lineage statements were found and none survived: the phrases that generate them, \"sibling of\" and \"class precedent\", assert similarity rather than derivation and correctly already carry related.\n\nThe consequence for this item's remaining scope. Ten honest precedence statements exist across a corpus spanning months and 14 releases, roughly one per two hundred items. Whatever remains of the terminal ordering deficit cannot be recovered by reading what is already written, because it was never written. That is not an argument for relaxing the evidence rules in this item's acceptance criteria; it is confirmation that they were the binding constraint all along, and that the lever is capture at write time rather than reconstruction after the fact."
  "2026-07-29T04:58:48.173Z","harness:claude-code","Active-graph enrichment pass 2026-07-29, with the refusal recorded because the refusal is the finding. Method: for every active item, compare the pm ids named in its own title, description, acceptance criteria and expected result against the ids it actually carries an edge to. Result: 73 active items name 181 ids they have no edge to. Eleven edges were written and 170 references were refused. What was written, all typed and all justified by the referencing item's own sentence: five consolidation tranches gained implements to the grammar ADR pm-pbyu because each names that ADR as the thing it delivers, one of them by retyping an existing related edge; pm-xkgq gained blocked_by pm-0z7n to match the eight sibling tranches whose stated foundation is the alias shim, replacing a related edge that asserted peerhood; pm-801d gained implements pm-usfg because its description states it lands as SDK primitives; pm-lixy gained verifies pm-4k6b because a graded judgment corpus is exactly the harness-proves-property shape; pm-lvyd gained blocked_by to pm-khdq, pm-zwib and pm-bgcu because its description names those three as prerequisites for meaningful throughput numbers. Why 170 were refused, one dominant class: the overwhelming majority of unedged id references in active items are BOUNDARY STATEMENTS, sentences of the form pm-X owns that, this item does not. Those sentences are the duplicate-check discipline working correctly, and converting a disclaimer into an edge would assert a relationship the author explicitly denied. This is a new refusal class, distinct from the three recorded on pm-ye46ns for the terminal ordering scan - hub-note misattribution, continuation over-reach, over-broad scope - and it is structural rather than accidental: the better the duplicate-check culture gets, the more unedged references appear, so this count will grow and must not be read as a deficit. Do not re-run this extraction expecting yield."
notes[4]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-25T17:59:47.662Z","harness:claude-code","Terminal-lineage baseline measured 2026-07-25 — the number this backfill exists to move, computed over all 2,027 item files by parsing declared dependency tables and parent fields directly (semantic edges are every declared dependency kind; hierarchy edges are the parent field).\n\nTerminal population: 1,772 closed or canceled items.\n\n- 832 (47%) carry zero semantic edges. By type: Task 238, Issue 232, Chore 169, Feature 126, Decision 28, Epic 16, Story 14, Plan 9.\n- 343 more carry exactly one semantic edge.\n- 1,013 (57.2%) have no semantic path to any of the six outcome milestones.\n- 254 have no path to an outcome milestone even through the parent hierarchy — these are the true orphans of the knowledge map, invisible to any traversal that starts from strategy.\n- Where lineage does exist it is genuinely deep: the terminal semantic depth histogram runs to 14 hops (1:8, 2:92, 3:163, 4:103, 5:98, 6:65, 7:75, 8:63, 9:50, 10:11, 11:10, 12:9, 13:9, 14:3), against a maximum of 6 for active items.\n\nThat last line is the argument for doing this work at all. History is where the deep structure already lives — the shipped lineage of a two-year-old decision reaches further than anything in the active set — and 47% of it is currently unreadable by a graph traversal. An agent asking \"why does this code exist\" or \"what did we already try here\" is answered by terminal items or not at all, which is exactly what pm-f86lth and the returning-agent story pm-bwyw depend on.\n\nBatch strategy implied by the measurement, for this item's execution:\n1. The 254 hierarchy-orphans first. They are the cheapest to fix (a parent or one typed edge each) and the highest value, because they are currently unreachable from every entry point, not merely under-linked.\n2. Then the 832 zero-edge set, epoch-ordered oldest-first, so each batch's changelog drag lands in one release window rather than smearing across many.\n3. The 343 single-edge items last; they are already reachable and gain the least per touch.\n\nChangelog constraint that shapes the batches, restated so it is not rediscovered: pm-changelog buckets closed items by `updated_at`, so every touched terminal item re-enters the current unreleased section. Batches must therefore be sized to what a single release note can honestly absorb, and each batch needs a changelog regeneration in the same commit. This is the reason the backfill cannot be done as one sweep, and the reason it was never done opportunistically during review passes."
  "2026-07-26T11:05:07.081Z","harness:claude-code","Terminal deficit baseline measured 2026-07-26 by parsing item files directly (the dependencies[N]{id,kind,...} table form; a grep for kind: \"implements\" finds nothing). Of 1,793 terminal items: 1,268 carry no outbound semantic edge, 1,041 carry no semantic edge in either direction, and 184 have no parent at all. Whole-workspace edge kinds for scale: related 2,411, implements 300, parent 275 as a dependency row, verifies 105, blocked_by 104, discovered_from 74, blocks 69, related_to 16, child 15, incident_from 1. Three consequences for the batch plan. First, 1,041 is the true size of the work, not 1,268 — an item reached by an active item's implements edge already participates in the graph even with no outbound edge of its own, so inbound coverage must be measured before a batch is sized. Second, the 184 parentless terminal items are the natural first epoch: they have no lineage at all, so any edge added is new information rather than a refinement, and they cannot be reached by hierarchy traversal from any root. Third, the 16 remaining related_to spellings are legacy vocabulary owned by pm-4020c5, and canonicalizing them during a terminal batch would mix two changes in one changelog window — leave them to their owner. This whole program is now explicitly blocked_by pm-pcys9n: with release attribution stable, these batches stop needing changelog regeneration as an atomic handoff and become ordinary work."
  "2026-07-26T22:22:14.586Z","harness:claude-code","Executed 2026-07-27: the epoch-lineage tranche. 214 typed edges written across 184 terminal items, zero failures, changelog-neutral because every touched item already carries a release stamp.\n\nDerivation rules, recorded so the result is auditable and reversible rather than trusted.\n\n1. Epoch roots. The 66 parentless terminal Epics are the project's delivery and review epochs, spanning 2026-02-17 to 2026-05-31. Each received one blocks edge to the index root, asserting only that the epoch preceded the post-v0.1 roadmap, which is true by construction for every closed epoch.\n\n2. The v1 epoch's internal structure. Each of the seven delivery Milestone epics received implements to its PRD counterpart and implements to the v1 build epic. The canonical v1 build epic received supersedes to the archived duplicate. The release-readiness epic received implements to the v1 build epic. Sixteen edges, all directly readable from the titles.\n\n3. The recurring review series. The sixteen dated audit and dogfood epics were chained by discovered_from in creation order, each successor pointing at its predecessor. This is a real causal series: each re-audit was prompted by the previous one.\n\n4. Parentless terminal non-Epics, 118 items. Attached by evidence in this precedence: an explicit id reference in the item's own prose naming an epoch root, then a shared release value with an epoch root created at or before it, then an exact same-day match with an epoch root. 48 matched and received discovered_from to that root. The 70 with no evidence received blocks to the index root rather than a guessed epoch, because a wrong lineage edge is worse than a shallow true one.\n\nMeasured effect on the whole graph.\n\n  connected components            16 -> 1\n  largest component          2,070 -> 2,116 (every node)\n  orphan nodes                     9 -> 0\n  terminal items with zero out-edge   158 -> 0\n  terminal items reaching the index root  820/1,805 -> 1,805/1,805\n  active items reaching the index root       308/308 (unchanged)\n  edges                       8,967 -> 9,232\n  max typed depth                 24 (unchanged); items at depth 7 or more roughly doubled\n  cycles                           8 (unchanged, all pre-existing legacy ordering cycles)\n  graph audit                     11 info findings, identical codes and subject counts\n\nNo related edges were written. The whole tranche is blocks, discovered_from, implements and one supersedes, so every new edge is traversable by the semantic verbs rather than only by the untyped bucket.\n\nWhat remains on this item: epoch partitioning as a first-class concept rather than a derived one, bounded resumability for future backfills, and the hub review now that the index root carries 136 inbound edges."
  "2026-07-28T22:44:10.335Z","harness:claude-code","Terminal-lineage state 2026-07-28, measured with a parser that handles BOTH on-disk dependency encodings. The correction matters: a parser written against the tabular shape alone reports seven epics as edgeless when each has four rows, and the two-shape divergence is now filed as pm-v3f1n4.\n\nINVARIANT REACHED. Every one of the 1,868 terminal items carries at least one typed semantic outbound edge. Zero-typed-out went to zero this pass by wiring the last member, pm-89neyq, whose body already stated the lineage it lacked in edges - it extends the guard pm-d7us placed on the later search scorer path, so supersedes was recorded, plus implements to the observability capability that surfaced it through Sentry.\n\nWhole-workspace edge census by kind, from the item documents themselves, 8,268 rows: related 5,107, implements 2,049, discovered_from 231, blocks 222, blocked_by 148, verifies 146, parent-as-dep-row 275, supersedes 55, child 15, related_to 16, incident_from 2. pm graph audit reports 9,818 edges over 2,185 nodes because it folds scalar parents and mirrored inverses into the same census; the two counts measure different things and neither is wrong.\n\nTwo observations for the epoch-partitioning work this item owns.\n\nThe 178 remaining parentless nodes are the real structural residual, and they are not a defect to be swept. Hierarchy depth distribution across all 2,182 items: 178 at depth 0, 374 at 1, 1,255 at 2, 335 at 3, 33 at 4, 6 at 5, 1 at 6. So the corpus is genuinely a wide forest of shallow programme trees rather than one deep tree, and that reflects how the project was actually run - a sequence of bounded remediation, dogfood and capability programmes, each rooted. Manufacturing hierarchy depth would encode a history that did not happen. The honest lever for making the timeline traversable is what this pass did instead: complete typed-edge coverage so semantic questions resolve by kind, plus epoch partitioning that groups the 178 roots by era rather than parenting them under an invented spine.\n\nrelated_to is still being minted, at 16 rows. That stays with pm-4020c5 and must not be folded into any terminal batch.\n\nThe v1 origin epoch is now reachable. pm-j7a, the programme root tagged milestone:root, went from 3 hierarchy descendants to 70 when the seven v1 milestones gained the scalar parent their dependency rows had already asserted. Evidence and the deliberately deferred ordering residual are recorded on pm-1w6scp; the direction defect that made those rows unreadable is pm-vk7zek."
body: "## Epoch ordering\n\nWork newest-to-oldest within stable lineages so current conventions inform, but do not overwrite, historical semantics. Suggested epochs: current release train; post-package architecture; SDK and extension expansion; early CLI and storage foundation; imported or legacy archives.\n\n## Evidence hierarchy\n\nExplicit item IDs and immutable mutation events outrank inferred title similarity. Linked commits, PRs, releases, tests, files, and docs provide provenance. Textual resemblance is only a candidate signal. Canceled experiments and superseded decisions remain visible and are not treated as outstanding work.\n\n## Changelog boundary\n\nTerminal metadata updates can move items into current generated changelog sections. Each bounded batch owns its final tracker mutation, package-owned changelog regeneration and check, diff review, and evidence comment as one atomic handoff."
